    "text": "Quick Summary The number five holds significant symbolic meaning across various cultures and belief systems. It is often seen as representing human life and marriage, stemming from the Pythagorean concept of 5 being the sum of the first even (2) and odd (3) numbers. The number is also associated with goddesses of love, such as Ishtar and Venus, whose symbol was the five-pointed star, or pentagram.1 In Manichaeism, five is central, with five elements of light and five of darkness, as well as five virtues and five vices. The Maya people also incorporated five into their cosmology, placing a fifth point at the center of the four compass points. The human hand with its five fingers and the body's five extremities (two arms, two legs, and the head) contribute to the mystique of the number five. In Islam, five is a sacred number, most notably represented by the Five Pillars of Islam and the five daily prayers.1 This summary is created from Britannica articles using AI. AI can make mistakes, so verify using Britannica articles. Ask AI about this topic Britannica doesn't have a full article on this topic, so we've curated a list of our relevant coverage. number symbolism In number symbolism: 5 The sum of the first even and odd numbers (2 + 3) is 5. (To the Pythagoreans 1 was not a number and was not odd.) It therefore symbolizes human life and—in the Platonic and Pythagorean traditions—marriage, as the sum of the female 2… Read More AI-generated answers from Britannica articles. AI makes mistakes, so verify using Britannica articles.",
